        <div id="what-makes-commercial" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Actually Makes a Zero Turn Mower Commercial
          </h2>

          <p>
            Commercial is a marketing word until you look under the seat. Every brand on this page also sells residential zero turns that look almost identical in a showroom photo, often in the same paint and with the same deck width. The difference is in five places a buyer rarely checks: what drives the wheels, how the deck is built, what class of engine is fitted, how the operator is isolated from the ground, and what the warranty says about hours. A commercial grade zero turn is a machine where all five are built for a mower that runs most of the working day, five or six days a week, for a whole season.
          </p>

          <p>
            The drive is the biggest single line. Residential mowers use sealed integrated transaxles such as Hydro-Gear's EZT family, which are not designed to be serviced and are replaced when they wear. Commercial mowers use either serviceable heavy-duty transaxles like the Hydro-Gear ZT-4400 and ZT-5400, or separate hydraulic pumps feeding wheel motors, the layout Scag and Hustler use. Both commercial approaches have filters and fluid you can change, and both are rated for far more hours than a homeowner will put on a mower.
          </p>

          <p>
            The deck is the second. Commercial decks are fabricated from welded plate, usually 7 gauge (about 0.18 inch) on the shell or skirt, and are deeper than stamped residential decks so they move more air through heavy grass. John Deere's 7-Iron PRO is the notable exception, a one-piece deck stamped from 7-gauge steel, which shows that thickness matters more than the stamped-versus-fabricated label. Spindles move to cast iron housings with tapered or larger bearings, and the front edge gets reinforcement against curbs and stumps.
          </p>

          <p>
            Engines move up to commercial lines: Kawasaki FX and FT, Kohler Command PRO, Vanguard BIG BLOCK, and on some machines a Kubota diesel or a propane engine. These have canister air cleaners, full-pressure lubrication and fuel injection options, and they are rated for heavy daily use. Finally, the warranty changes shape. A residential mower might carry three years and 300 hours; the commercial machines here list terms like five years or 1,200 hours with no hour limit for the first two years, which tells you what duty cycle the maker expects.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT VERSION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Check the drive, the deck gauge and the warranty hours before the horsepower.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A sealed EZT transaxle, a 10-gauge stamped deck and a 300-hour warranty mean residential, whatever the decal says. Serviceable ZT-4400/ZT-5400 transaxles or pumps and wheel motors, 7-gauge deck steel and a warranty measured in 1,000-plus hours mean commercial.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

