        <div id="drive-systems" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Transaxles vs Pumps and Wheel Motors
          </h2>

          <p>
            There are two commercial drive layouts on this list, and the choice between them is the first real fork in a commercial mower purchase. The first is an integrated transaxle: pump, motor and gearing in one sealed housing at each rear wheel, but built heavier than residential units and designed to be serviced. Hydro-Gear's ZT-4400 on the Ferris ISX 2200 and ZT-5400 on the Gravely Pro-Turn 260 are the common examples. They have external filters, changeable fluid and cooling fans, and they are simpler and cheaper to build than a pump-and-motor system.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second layout splits the job. A pair of hydraulic pumps sits near the engine, and hoses or rigid lines carry fluid to a wheel motor at each drive wheel. The Scag Turf Tiger II uses two 16cc Hydro-Gear pumps with two 18 cubic inch Parker wheel motors; the Hustler Super Z uses larger Hydro-Gear 21cc PY pumps with Parker TG wheel motors and stainless Seal-Loc lines. Splitting the components lets each be sized independently, puts the heat-generating pump in moving air, and makes it possible to replace a wheel motor without touching the pump.
          </p>

          <p>
            Exmark and Kubota sit in between. Exmark's X-Series uses Parker unitized pump and wheel motor systems with no hoses, and Kubota's Z700 uses Parker HTG14 transmissions that integrate a pump and motor in each unit. These designs keep the serviceability and heavy internals while removing the hose fittings that are the classic leak point on older pump-and-motor mowers.
          </p>

          <p>
            What does this mean for a buyer? For a solo operator or a homeowner with ten or fifteen acres, a ZT-4400 or ZT-5400 machine is enough drivetrain, and it is usually the cheaper way into a commercial mower. For a crew machine that will log 600 to 1,000 hours a year, a pump-and-motor or unitized Parker system is the safer long-term bet, and it is what the most expensive mowers on this list use. Either way, the hydraulic fluid and filter change at the interval in the operator's manual is the most important maintenance item on the machine.
          </p>

