        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best commercial zero turn mower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For full-time crew use, the Scag Turf Tiger II is our top pick for its pump-and-wheel-motor drive, heavy Velocity Plus deck and wide engine choice. The Gravely Pro-Turn 260 is the best value, pairing Hydro-Gear ZT-5400 transaxles and a 7-gauge fabricated deck with a five-year, 1,250-hour warranty. The Ferris ISX 2200 rides best thanks to its suspended chassis.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What makes a zero turn mower commercial grade?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Look for a serviceable heavy-duty drive, either Hydro-Gear ZT-4400 or ZT-5400 class transaxles or separate pumps and wheel motors, a deck built from 7-gauge steel or reinforced plate, a commercial engine such as a Kawasaki FX or Vanguard BIG BLOCK, a suspension seat, and a warranty written in 1,000-plus hours rather than a few hundred.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many hours does a commercial zero turn mower last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Manufacturers do not publish a lifespan, but their warranties show what they expect: Hustler and Gravely write five-year terms of 1,200 and 1,250 hours, and John Deere three years or 1,200 hours. Owners commonly report commercial machines running well beyond those figures when hydraulic fluid, filters and engine oil are changed on schedule.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a pump and wheel motor drive better than a ZT-5400 transaxle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For very high hours, pump-and-motor systems like Scag's and Hustler's are easier to service piece by piece and are what the most expensive mowers use. For an owner-operator or a homeowner, a ZT-5400 is a serviceable commercial unit that will rarely be worn out, and it keeps the machine's price several thousand dollars lower.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you buy a commercial zero turn mower on Amazon?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not new from the major commercial brands. Scag, Exmark, Hustler, Gravely, Ferris, John Deere and Kubota sell commercial zero turns through authorized dealers, who handle delivery, setup and warranty service. Amazon listings under those brand names are almost always parts, blades and covers, not the mowers.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a commercial zero turn worth it for a homeowner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Above about five acres of regular mowing, or on rough rural ground, often yes: a 7-gauge deck, a suspension seat and a commercial drive make long mows easier and last for decades. Under four acres, a good residential or prosumer machine does the job for a fraction of the price, and the commercial durability goes unused.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a commercial zero turn mower cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most brands only quote through dealers. Two that publish prices give a sense of the range: Kubota USA lists the Z781KWTi-60 from $14,849, and Exmark's site lists $20,199 for the 60 inch Lazer Z X-Series LRX921GKA606Q1. Entry commercial machines from Gravely and Ferris are typically quoted below the Exmark.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

